WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:06.210
I'm now going to introduce an
ancient method for generating

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.400
a list of primes
up to some limit

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.070
N called the Sieve
of Eratosthenes.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.940
Now, Eratosthenes
was born in 276 BC,

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:22.940
so this method is
over 2,200 years old,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.170
but it's very
simple and elegant,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.080
and you could teach
it to any child.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.900
Now let's say, for
example, we want

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.030
to calculate all the
primes up to 100.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.500
But this would work
in the same way

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:37.410
if we wanted to calculate
up to 10,000 or a billion.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.530
It proceeds as follows.

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:41.870
Assume all numbers are unmarked.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.260
Gray is unmarked.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.120
We start at the
beginning, and if we

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:49.080
find a number that is
unmarked, we know it's prime.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:53.250
So we hit 2, and we say 2 is
prime because it's unmarked.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.480
And then the second
phase is now we

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.730
can eliminate all
multiples of 2,

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:00.530
because we know
they're composite.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.830
So bam, we jump along and we
eliminate all multiples of 2.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.450
Red means composite.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:07.730
And now we repeat.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:10.240
We step along from 2 to 3.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.940
3 is unmarked, so
we mark 3 as prime.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.590
And now we can eliminate
all multiples of 3.

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.550
And a really simple
optimization is

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:20.450
notice 6 is already crossed off.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:22.090
We actually cross
off the multiples

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.510
starting at the
square of that number.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.420
So 3 times 3 is 9.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:32.220
We start at 9 and mark all
multiples of 3 as composite.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.030
And again, we go back.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:35.596
We jump along to 4.

00:01:35.596 --> 00:01:37.530
Well, 4 is marked, we
know it's composite,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:38.650
and we jump along to 5.

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:40.240
We found an unmarked number.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.170
5 is prime.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:44.310
Now, 5 times 5 is 25.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:49.630
We go to 25, we mark off 25,
30, 35, 40, 45, and so on.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:50.910
Those are composites.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.110
We proceed forward
until we hit 7.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:55.800
We know 7 is prime
because it's unmarked.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.260
7 times 7 is 49.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.370
We mark 49 and all multiples
of 7 above it as composite.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.350
And now we move along
until we hit 11.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.220
11 is prime.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.000
And notice now, 11 times
11 is greater than 100,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.480
so we can actually
stop at this point.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.550
We could have
stopped at 10 even,

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.200
because now all remaining
unmarked numbers,

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.930
if you'll notice, are prime.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.920
And we could in one swoop
mark them all as prime.

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:28.380
And that's the whole algorithm.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.850
It's so simple.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:33.240
And just to generalize how we
do this, so we could write up

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.430
a program to perform
this sieve, is

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:40.730
if we want to find all
primes up to some number N,

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:42.530
we first create a main loop.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:48.300
So we have 4, all numbers a
from 2 to the square root of n.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.060
So notice here we stopped.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:53.200
When we hit 10, or
I showed it as 11,

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.670
we actually stop because
we have found all primes.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:01.290
So from 2 to the square root
of N, we proceed as follows.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:08.800
If a is unmarked, then
we know a is prime,

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.590
and when we find
a prime number, we

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:15.820
mark all multiples of
a off as composite.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:16.610
And that's it.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.610
So you find a number prime,
mark off the multiples,

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:23.480
go back to the beginning,
increment a by one.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:28.940
So we're on 2, then we go to 3,
then we go to 4, 5, and so on.

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:32.550
And when we're done,
we have all primes.

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.930
Notice here that
this is also a loop.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:38.370
So we have a main loop 4,
and when we find a prime,

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.190
this marking off of
multiples is also a loop.

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:45.220
So it's important to notice
here that we have a nested loop.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.860
We have a loop inside a loop.

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.530
Here is an example of
this algorithm in action--

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.620
I wrote in JavaScript
below-- if I put in 100, here

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.690
are all the primes under 100.

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:58.860
And if I put in 200, here
are all the primes under 200.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:06.170
And if I put in 850, here
are all the primes under 850.

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:09.890
And below, I have this program
with a recording explaining

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.150
how I set it up.

